#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134817} :activity/id #uuid "3a450e6c-560e-5c98-a021-778f5de2c088" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050841 :topic/id #uuid "6aced1c3-bd0e-5b1d-973e-105b955f9e1a" :topic/math-academy-id 2126} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134817 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135105 :tutorial/content "Let $V$ be an inner product space with the inner product $\\langle\\cdot, \\cdot\\rangle.$ If \n\n$$\n\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\n is a set of mutually orthogonal vectors in $V,$  then the **orthogonal projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$ onto the subspace \n\n$$\nS = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}\n$$\n\nwith respect to the inner product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+⋯+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{n}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,{a}_{1}⟩}{⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{1}⟩}{a}_{1}+\\frac{⟨x,{a}_{2}⟩}{⟨{a}_{2},{a}_{2}⟩}{a}_{2}+⋯+\\frac{⟨x,{a}_{n}⟩}{⟨{a}_{n},{a}_{n}⟩}{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $S$ is the sum of the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto each one-dimensional subspace $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_i\\}$ for $i=1, 2, \\cdots, n.$ \n\nNote the following:\n\n- This is entirely analogous to the corresponding formula for Euclidean spaces.\n\n- As in Euclidean spaces, the formula works *only* when the set of vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is orthogonal with respect to the inner product!" :tutorial/id #uuid "9b462101-527b-5e26-bcd4-89b8459552bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5162 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "91e83dab-899c-5474-8059-ebdcc7a5278a" :step/math-academy-id 32457 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134818 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133873 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133689 :question/id #uuid "18068394-b64b-5bc8-a2da-b0db4085ecba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8729"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df300955-830a-5347-9aeb-2b2b25f865c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048893}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240962}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "4240c631-050e-58b5-968f-46d616a5f794" :step/math-academy-id 32458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134819}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133874 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133690 :question/id #uuid "4e4a5db0-2ea0-542e-abdd-c5b3abfe2134" :question/math-academy-id "e-8730"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050009}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241049}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "e60ded4d-fa5d-5393-8f16-7c1cc6abe2dd" :step/math-academy-id 32459 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134820}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135106 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $V$ is a vector space equipped with an inner product $\\left\\langle\\cdot, \\cdot\\right\\rangle$ and that $\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in V.$ The **distance $d$ between $\\mathbf v$ and $\\mathbf w$ with respect to the inner product** is defined by the *norm* of $\\mathbf{v}- \\mathbf{w}.$ That is,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖v-w‖=\\sqrt{⟨v-w,v-w⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that $V = \\mathcal{C}[0,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\int_0^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe distance between the functions $f(t)=4t$ and $g(t)=t+1$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖4t-(t+1)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t-1‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨3t-1,3t-1⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (3t-1)^{3}|}_{0}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot [(8)-(-1)]} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMore generally, given an orthogonal set \n\n$$\n\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\} \\subset  V\n$$\n\nand a vector $\\mathbf x \\in V,$  the **distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace** \n\n$$\nS=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,  \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\n\\Vert{ \\color{black} \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} }\\Vert.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ca26c36-a2e4-51d5-ae7d-a4b59234cedb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5163 :tutorial/title "The Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "7d1a7540-bb8b-5865-a0e0-852beed15e26" :step/math-academy-id 32460 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134821}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133875 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133691 :question/id #uuid "d7541321-4042-54af-9b64-59f255a6f1a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049274}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375652}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "20a627a4-468b-5c73-a4aa-e3ab9c87207e" :step/math-academy-id 32485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134828}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134828 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135107 :tutorial/content "The orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ (or simply onto a vector $\\mathbf{a}$) is the vector $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x}\\in \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ that is \"closest\" to $\\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the distance defined by the inner product.\n\nAlthough we've considered abstract vector spaces with any possible inner product in this lesson, we can still visualize this situation with the following diagram:\n\n![](images/09/099a0bb20552824d2376994c3eb7145e48bbf78552d16de39fec44bda6b766f2.png)\n\nAs shown in the diagram, the projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ is the vector $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x}$ such that $\\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}.$\n\nFurthermore, $\\| \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\|$ will be the smallest possible distance (with respect to our inner product) from $\\mathbf{x}$ to any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "629bc20f-76c3-5217-b763-e4ab0c831a8d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5343 :tutorial/title "The Intuition Behind Orthogonal Projections in Inner Product Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "97501e93-7a73-5f78-8d81-1f3d7a340746" :step/math-academy-id 33420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135141 :tutorial/content "Consider the functions \n\n$$\nf_1(t)=t,\\qquad f_2(t)=t^2+1, \\qquad h(t)= 5t^3,\n$$\n\nin the vector space $V = \\mathcal{C}[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1],$ where the inner product is defined as\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\int_{-1}^1 x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's find the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto the subspace\n\n$$\nS= \\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t), f_2(t)\\},\n$$\n\n \n\ngiven that $f_1(t) \\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to this inner product. \n\nSince the set $\\{f_1(t),f_2(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,h(t) \n= \\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,h(t) + \\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,h(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute these projections:\n\n- First, we compute  the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t)\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,h(t) & =\\frac{⟨h(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{1}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot t \\\\  & =3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we compute  the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_2(t)\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,h(t) & =\\frac{⟨h(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{2}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote: We used the fact that the integrand in the numerator is an odd function, so its integral over $[-1,1]$ must be zero.\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,h(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,h(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,h(t) \\\\  & =3t+0 \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "975c7894-a67f-5528-875e-2e55a94d99b9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6130 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "b89a94ac-0dd4-505c-b266-41783d527047" :step/math-academy-id 37245 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134818}}] :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Inner Product Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134005}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413797} :activity/id #uuid "fba5b1da-9880-515c-af94-05681b35cd3b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050841 :topic/id #uuid "6aced1c3-bd0e-5b1d-973e-105b955f9e1a" :topic/math-academy-id 2126} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413793 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a97740-bf4e-5245-a223-1105ad76868c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238086} {:answer/id #uuid "0ccb496a-1f3e-5f9c-9be5-7756bb97a385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238169} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbfd16e-b04d-5762-91cf-d69f6af7923d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239432} {:answer/id #uuid "a5578604-9539-5565-8bbb-579ff2d23c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239490} {:answer/id #uuid "ff74f82e-113d-5ba7-9d63-962e019ecfd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238086} :answer-field/id #uuid "50e2f23b-8197-5ffd-805f-f3ecff761a73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e7a8c44b-b300-5e66-9428-e03f400ebf5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105416" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,g(t)={sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}$ equals $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{1}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while ${sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0a412cdd-29f8-5273-ab55-2a8dce391dae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413795}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413794 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a9db971-884b-5cf9-9a84-c91c9fa4fa01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238276} {:answer/id #uuid "39658250-b0fe-59bb-a334-a4e517b0d75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238550} {:answer/id #uuid "880b7d31-ec04-56db-ad2a-f754621f1b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239237} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b15087-e383-5255-8efc-84ad43c168b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239607} {:answer/id #uuid "cd180c30-2f84-5a24-af62-8965f36db06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239851}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239607} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c982ce-3e72-5b74-bcb0-6eb543685681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2bf55fb2-ca86-5707-a52a-d891899878f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-105186" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=2,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $6.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=6,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+6{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have integrals over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) & =\\frac{-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =6-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0bc7e18b-fdb5-5429-828f-e671a9abcbda" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413793}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413795 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "694bbe7e-1033-5685-8db7-c579e340a4af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238969} {:answer/id #uuid "7187a2ab-aec2-5516-9817-82c6b1e6f0ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239028} {:answer/id #uuid "98f9f087-a4cb-5b2b-ba88-6c34977c1ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239383} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b7eedf-e87f-53c1-a7c0-62ebe727a2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239882} {:answer/id #uuid "f69f76e8-1b93-5cd1-b517-2cdfc888efb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239028} :answer-field/id #uuid "eabf99e8-310f-5528-b7c0-2d81c99774f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a70f1b99-0d4b-5ef2-9e7e-0dc7f6d78417" :question/math-academy-id "q-105461" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t-(-3t+3)‖ \\\\  & =‖6t-3‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-36t+9)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}-18{t}^{2}+9t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-18+9)-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "5b37fdbb-b243-534e-930c-c4161c64569a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413796}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413796 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e4eb1a-f07b-5500-9b6b-bb104419f1ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238193} {:answer/id #uuid "1597005a-083a-5b2f-904c-d3601ce7fd24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238235} {:answer/id #uuid "189191f2-31ca-5b6a-bb47-311abb8904ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238264} {:answer/id #uuid "46644984-af18-5419-96cd-d6307758e813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238666} {:answer/id #uuid "94691432-7499-5d85-bd53-7c03102b3e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238264} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7bf2326-7e5c-5bac-b442-4f7e564308dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d460b9a-6ed3-5d23-955f-f3e1840b55a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105475" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=8t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=4t-2$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}8t\\cdot (4t-2)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4t-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(32{t}^{2}-16t)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-16t+4)\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{[\\frac{32}{3}{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1}}{[\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+4t]_{0}^{1}}\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{32}{3}-8)-0}{(\\frac{16}{3}-4)-0}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{3})}{(\\frac{4}{3})}\\cdot (4t+2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =8t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $f(t)=8t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=4t-2$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖8t-(8t-4)‖ \\\\  & =‖4‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨4,4⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨4,4⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =16-0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{16}=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "5b4d4a19-f8e8-5a9a-8c13-582c4e2b0863"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413797 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b29cd5-55b5-5722-ba90-23f769345723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238150} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc19ba5-b098-5af2-b759-18b7d61fa0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238854} {:answer/id #uuid "64e8e822-ab38-5fcf-a7cb-162397992404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238937} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd846ec-dcb9-5576-8a82-5cbd0e68c90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239418} {:answer/id #uuid "a19047b8-6624-5f0c-b217-a2d6a5d4fcd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239462}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238937} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a8c5d5-fcb2-5bdd-8555-14fa5c61c1d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "645a82fe-e42f-5dc9-9e79-d8bd0747a894" :question/math-academy-id "q-105136" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,g(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "681a7c6c-5a02-51e9-b790-4836129a3fb4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413794}}] :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Inner Product Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413792}]}
